 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Rasterization remains $O(N)$ per pixel where $N$ is the number of overlapping Gaussians, similar to standard 3DGS. The additional cost is the VLM text encoding (offline) and the similarity computation during inference.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Increases by $O(N \cdot d)$ to store feature vectors $\mathbf{f}_i$ for each Gaussian. For CLIP ViT-L/14 ($d=768$), this adds significant memory overhead compared to RGB (3 channels).
-*   **Strengths:** Enables real-time open-vocabulary segmentation and phrase grounding without retraining for new categories (zero-shot).
-*   **Limitations:** Requires a VLM for inference; semantic quality depends on the alignment between 3D features and the VLM space; potential for semantic bleeding at boundaries.
-*   **When to use:** When semantic understanding, object retrieval, or phrase grounding is required in a 3D scene.
-*   **When not to use:** When memory is constrained or only photometric accuracy is needed.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Rasterization is $O(N)$ per pixel where $N$ is the number of overlapping Gaussians, same as standard 3DGS. The additional cost is the dimensionality of the feature vector $\mathbf{f}_i$ during accumulation and the similarity computation against text embeddings.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage increases by the size of the feature vector $\mathbf{f}_i$ per Gaussian. For CLIP embeddings (e.g., 512 dims), this is significantly larger than color (3 dims). Not given in the sources.
+*   **Strengths:** Enables real-time open-vocabulary semantic segmentation and phrase grounding without retraining the segmentation head for new classes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires a pre-trained vision-language model for text encoding. Semantic quality depends on the alignment between the 3DGS feature space and the VLM embedding space. Not given in the sources.
 
 ## Background
-Language Embedded 3D Gaussians (LE3DG) was introduced as an extension to 3D Gaussian Splatting to incorporate semantic understanding [S1, unofficial]. It builds upon the principle that feature vectors can be differentially accumulated during rasterization. The method enables tasks like 3D visual question answering and zero-shot semantic reasoning by projecting vision-language models into the 3DGS framework [S1, unofficial]. It is part of a broader trend of integrating VLMs with neural radiance fields and Gaussian splatting for embodied AI and robotics applications.
+Language Embedded 3D Gaussians (LE3DG) was introduced as an expansion of feature blending to full scene understanding, encoding object-level embeddings for phrase grounding [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the foundation of 3D Gaussian Splatting with Feature Vectors [8060-3d-gaussian-splatting-with-feature-vectors.md] and integrates with the broader family of semantic and language embeddings [8130-semantic-and-language-embeddings.md]. The method is cited in the context of enabling open-vocabulary semantic segmentation in real time by comparing rendered feature maps against CLIP text embeddings [S1, unofficial].
 
